<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Imperialism, the Newest Stage of Capitalism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">Imperialism</a>, the Highest Stage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">Capitalism</a> is a book by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a>, written in Zürich in 1916 and published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> in 1917, originally as Imperialism, the Newest Stage of Capitalism. It argues that capitalism had moved from competition to monopoly, that the merger of bank and industrial capital produced finance capital and a financial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>, and that the export of capital to undeveloped countries divided the world among the great powers. Lenin presented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> as a predatory war among empires. He drew on Imperialism: A Study (1902) by John A. Hobson and Finance Capital (1910) by Rudolf Hilferding, and rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-kautsky">Karl Kautsky</a>'s theory of ultra-imperialism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imperialism-the-highest-stage-of-capitalism.jpg" alt="Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism" width="330" height="492" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Filimonov Ivan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ленин_В._И._Империализм,_как_высшая_стадия_капитализма_(1917).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vladimir Lenin, drawing on Rudolf Hilferding’s Finance Capital (1910), called the banks “the chief nerve centres” of the capitalist system in <b>Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism</b> (1917).</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Vladimir Lenin built on Hobson in <b>Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism</b> (1916), portraying imperialism as monopoly capitalism on a world scale that made imperialist wars inevitable; the treatise remained a standard textbook until the Soviet collapse.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/imperialism">Imperialism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
